# Trivy misconfiguration exclusions — deliberate, project-level policy exceptions.
#
# Format: one AVD/CVE ID per line. Every entry MUST carry a comment stating the
# decision, the policy it follows, and the condition that would revoke it.
#
# Per the workspace AGENTS.md section "Scan findings — fix the root cause;
# suppression is the last resort", this is a TIER-2 exception: a systemic,
# legitimate pattern encoded ONCE at project level, rather than a scattered
# inline suppression. House style matches
# platform/ffreis-platform-shared-infra/.trivyignore.
#
# This file is trivy's default ignorefile: it is auto-loaded from the working
# directory, so `make security`'s `trivy config --exit-code 1 --severity
# HIGH,CRITICAL .` (run from the repo root) picks it up with no extra flag.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AVD-AWS-0132 (HIGH) — "Bucket does not encrypt data with a customer managed key."
#
# DELIBERATE: none of the tf-state buckets in terraform/stack/state.tf use
# SSE-KMS with a customer managed key (CMK).
#
# Rationale — the fleet no-KMS cost rule. Workspace AGENTS.md § "Fixed-cost
# discipline" lists `aws_kms_key` at $1/month EACH and names "AES256 / AWS-owned
# key" as the sanctioned alternative. The 2026-07 fleet KMS audit independently
# confirmed this AWS account holds zero customer-managed KMS keys account-wide
# ($0.00 CMK spend) — so by definition none of these buckets, or anything else
# in the account, can be using a CMK. The sibling `tf_state_runtime` bucket
# earlier in the same file (created via the shared s3-bucket module) makes the
# same choice explicitly (`sse_algorithm = "AES256"`).
#
# Buckets covered — aws_s3_bucket.tf_state_root / tf_state_prod / tf_state_dev
# in terraform/stack/state.tf. These three are *adopted* resources (imported
# from the ffreis-platform-bootstrap CLI, which pre-dates this Terraform
# stack): only tags are managed here, so no encryption/versioning/public-access
# resource appears in this stack's HCL for them at all — trivy has no config to
# read either way. What the bootstrap CLI's `EnsureStateBucket` actually
# configures is out of scope for this ignore entry; it is scoped narrowly to
# the *key-ownership* question (CMK vs AWS-owned), which the account-wide zero-
# CMK fact answers regardless of which repo owns the bucket's other settings.
#
# REVISIT IF: a customer-managed KMS key is ever provisioned in this account,
# or any of these buckets starts holding customer PII, credentials, or
# regulated data (state buckets hold Terraform state, not customer data).
AVD-AWS-0132

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AVD-AWS-0086 / 0087 / 0091 / 0093 (HIGH) — the four S3 public-access-block
# checks: block public ACLs, block public policy, ignore public ACLs, restrict
# public buckets.
#
# FALSE POSITIVE IN CONTEXT, not an accepted risk. All four settings ARE
# enabled on all three buckets; trivy simply cannot see them, because they are
# not configured in this stack's HCL.
#
# Why they are not in the HCL: aws_s3_bucket.tf_state_root / tf_state_prod /
# tf_state_dev (terraform/stack/state.tf) are *adopted* resources, imported from
# the ffreis-platform-bootstrap CLI which pre-dates this stack. As the header
# comment on that file states, Terraform deliberately manages ONLY tags for
# them; versioning, encryption and public-access-block stay owned by the CLI,
# with `ignore_changes` preventing drift. Adding
# `aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block` resources here to silence trivy would
# create a SECOND owner for a setting the CLI already manages — a worse outcome
# than the warning, and a direct contradiction of the documented design.
#
# Verified twice on 2026-07-31, not assumed:
#   1. Source — ffreis-platform-bootstrap internal/aws/s3.go `blockPublicAccess`
#      sets BlockPublicAcls, BlockPublicPolicy, IgnorePublicAcls and
#      RestrictPublicBuckets all to true, called via EnsureStateBucket
#      (internal/bootstrap/bootstrap.go).
#   2. Live AWS — `aws s3api get-public-access-block` on ffreis-tf-state-root,
#      -prod and -dev each returned all four settings true.
#
# REVISIT IF: ownership of these buckets moves from the bootstrap CLI into this
# Terraform stack (then declare the blocks in HCL and DELETE these four lines),
# or if a `get-public-access-block` audit ever returns anything but all-true.
AVD-AWS-0086
AVD-AWS-0087
AVD-AWS-0091
AVD-AWS-0093
